diff --git a/components/thoron_update/README.md b/components/thoron_update/README.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..35d8d4d6e1b99357d973bdac332fbba4c22f656c
--- /dev/null
+++ b/components/thoron_update/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,220 @@
+# thoron_update
+
+The core of Thoron's updater (ROADMAP P3.3): what a copy of Thoron makes of the two files a release
+publishes, `update.json` and `update.json.sig`.
+
+The format, the order of the checks and the reason for each are in `docs/update/MANIFEST.md` of Thoron's
+repository. `tools/update/manifest.py` there is the reference: `CheckUpdate()` here gives the answer its
+`check_update()` gives, for every input. Where the two differ, that is a bug in one of them.
+
+## What it does
+
+`CheckUpdate(manifest, signature, copy, now)` in `manifest.h` takes the two files as fetched, what the copy
+knows about itself (`CopyInfo`: the public key, the allowed hosts, its channel, the installed version, the
+updater's version, its variant and kind, the highest serial it has accepted) and the clock. In this order:
+
+1. An empty file, or one of more than 262144 bytes, is `malformed`.
+2. The Ed25519 signature (BoringSSL) is checked over the purpose prefix and the exact bytes, with a key that
+   is a key: all zero and the other points of small order are none. Nothing of the manifest is read before.
+3. The same buffer is read as JSON, held to the schema, and written out again: only the one written form of
+   the spec gives the same bytes. That refuses repeated keys, other spacing and other spellings of a
+   number, whatever the JSON reader makes of them.
+4. The copy decides: channel, serial, dates (the clock may be off by 24 hours), version, updater version,
+   package.
+
+The answer is one `Decision`: an `Outcome`, and only when the manifest is the channel's current word, its
+content, the serial to store and, with an update, the full package and the delta from exactly the
+installed version.
+
+| `Outcome` | Meaning | About page |
+|---|---|---|
+| `kUpdate` | A newer release with a package for this copy | Downloading, then ready to relaunch |
+| `kUpToDate` | The current manifest names the installed version | Up to date |
+| `kBadSignature` | The signature does not hold, or there is no key. Nothing was read | Cannot verify |
+| `kMalformed` | Empty, too long, or signed and still not a schema 1 manifest | Cannot verify |
+| `kWrongChannel` | A manifest of another channel | Cannot verify |
+| `kReplayed` | Older than a manifest this copy has accepted | Cannot verify |
+| `kNotYetValid` | Dated after the clock | Cannot verify, with a hint to check the date and time |
+| `kExpired` | Too old to show what the newest release is | Cannot verify |
+| `kDowngrade` | Names a version below the installed one | Cannot verify |
+| `kUpdaterTooOld` | A newer release this updater may not apply | Installs like an update when the decision has the full package: the updater inside it applies the release (P4.6); else its own message |
+| `kNoPackage` | A newer release with nothing for this variant and kind | Its own message |
+| `kInvalidCopy` | The caller's own values make no sense; the files were not looked at | Error |
+
+`CannotVerify()` is true for the seven from `kBadSignature` to `kDowngrade`. Only `kUpToDate` is ever shown
+as up to date. `kInvalidCopy` is not in the spec: the reference raises an error there, this code has no
+exceptions.
+
+## What a caller does
+
+- Reads at most 262145 bytes of `update.json` and hands over the bytes as fetched, never a second read.
+- Stores `remember_serial`, when there is one, as the highest serial accepted on the channel: outside the
+  profile, and only when it is higher than the stored one.
+- Uses a downloaded file only after its length is `size` and its SHA-256 is `sha256`.
+
+## What else it holds
+
+- `package.h`: a downloaded file is used only when its length and SHA-256 are the manifest's (P3.4); the
+  delta is tried before the full package when it is smaller, and the full package is the fallback (P4.2);
+  the build that fits the processor (P4.4).
+- `swap.h`: a version is put in place in a folder of its own and switched to by one atomic write of the
+  file `current`, which names the version that starts and the one before it; a kill at any step leaves a
+  version that starts (P3.5); a rollback switches back (P3.8).
+- `snapshot.h`: a verified copy of what matters in every profile, checked by SQLite, the last two kept, and
+  its restore (P3.7, P3.8).
+- `cli.h`: the command line, `thoron_update.exe`, and the update modes (P3.11).
+- `launcher/`: `Thoron.exe` of the portable package (P3.12).
+- `setup/`: `ThoronSetup.exe`, the one installer for good (P3.19): it fetches the channel's signed manifest
+  with Windows' own HTTP client (https only, no cookies, the system's proxy), checks it as every updater does,
+  picks the fastest build the processor runs (`setup_plan.h`: AVX2, then SSE4; a release whose
+  `min_updater_version` is above its own build stops it), downloads that release's full installer, checks it
+  on the disk against the signed manifest and runs it. The same file serves every release. Its build for
+  tests (`thoron_setup_for_tests`, never shipped) trusts only the published test key and takes the release
+  from a folder; `tools/qa/thoron_setup_check.py` runs it, and `release.py` ships `ThoronSetup.exe` only when
+  it holds none of the test build's switches.
+- `update_client.h`: the browser's own updater (P3.20, P3.9): fetches `update.json`, `update.json.sig` and
+  `update.key` beside it (no cookies, no cache, https only, redirects included), decides with
+  `CheckUpdateWithKeys`, keeps the highest accepted serial and the key statements that lead to a trusted
+  key in `Local State`, downloads the delta first when it is smaller and then the full package, checks
+  every download with `PlaceVerifiedFile` and hands the checked package to the caller's installer. A
+  download that breaks keeps what arrived (`<sha256>.part`, also across restarts) and goes on with an HTTP
+  range for the rest, up to 3 attempts; a server that sends it all again is fine, a range that starts
+  elsewhere is never appended, and a resumed file that does not verify is fetched once more whole (P4.3).
+  A look on a schedule downloads nothing on a metered connection; one the person asked for does. Once a
+  version is installed it tells the browser (`on_installed`), which shows the relaunch badge (P3.6). Each
+  step is a state for the About page (`chrome/browser/ui/webui/help/version_updater_thoron_win.cc`; the
+  copy's values are in `chrome/browser/thoron_update/`). Without a key it asks nothing. Its tests serve a
+  release through Chromium's fake network (`update_client_test_data.h`, made by
+  `make_client_test_data.py` from the reference tool).
+
+## The command line
+
+    thoron_update --root <program folder> --status
+    thoron_update --root <program folder> --apply <staged folder> --version <v> --files <files.json>
+        [--handed-off]
+    thoron_update --root <program folder> --apply-package <portable .zip> --version <v>
+    thoron_update --root <program folder> --apply-delta <file.delta> --version <v>
+    thoron_update --root <Application folder> --apply-installed-delta <file.delta> --from <v> --version <v>
+        [--system-level]
+    thoron_update --root <program folder> --rollback
+    thoron_update --root <program folder> --mode [auto|notify|off]
+    thoron_update --check --manifest <update.json> --signature <update.json.sig> --public-key <base64>
+        --channel <channel> --installed <v> --updater <v> --variant <name> --kind installed|portable
+        --allowed-host <host> [--last-serial <n>] [--now <seconds since 1970>]
+
+`files.json` is a list of `{"path", "size", "sha256"}`, one for each file of the version, with `/` between
+the parts of a path. `--check` reads the two files of a release as they were fetched.
+
+**The release's own updater applies the release (P4.6).** When the staged folder holds `thoron_update.exe`,
+`--apply` copies it into the program folder as `thoron_update.handoff.exe` (the staged folder is renamed
+during the swap, so nothing may run from it), checks the copy's length and SHA-256 against its entry in
+`files.json`, and runs it with the same command line and `--handed-off`; that one applies the release and
+its output and exit code are passed on, after the line `handed off to the updater of <version>`. A copy that
+does not match its entry is never started: the apply ends with exit 12 and the staged folder goes. So an
+updater below a release's `min_updater_version` only fetches, checks and unpacks the package (`--check`
+then names its full package, never a delta), and never runs the swap of a release it may not apply.
+`tools/qa/updater_handoff_check.py` checks this with the real program.
+
+**A portable copy's update (P3.20).** `--apply-package` takes the release's portable `.zip`
+(`tools/release/portable.py`), already checked against the signed manifest, and unpacks only
+`ThoronPortable/Application/<v>/` into `<v>.staging` of the program folder (`portable_package.h`: a link, an
+encrypted entry, a name that cannot be made safe, another top folder, over 4 GiB or 20000 files refuse the
+whole package and leave nothing), lists what it wrote and applies it as `--apply` does, through the updater in
+it. Then it puts the package's `Thoron.exe` over the launcher beside the program folder when it differs
+(written beside it, then moved over it). The browser of a portable copy runs this as its installer
+(`chrome/browser/thoron_update/`), and its relaunch starts the version `current` names
+(`BrowserOfCurrentVersion` in `swap.h`). `tools/qa/portable_update_check.py` checks it with a real package.
+
+**Deltas (P4.1).** `--apply-delta` makes the new version's folder from the current one's with a delta
+of `tools/release/delta.py` (`delta_package.h`): every file of the new folder is the older file as it is,
+made from it with a Zucchini patch (Chromium's `components/zucchini`, the code the tool made it with), or
+whole. Every older file is checked against the SHA-256 the delta names before it is used and every file
+made against the new one's; a path that leaves the folder, a delta for other versions or a patch that does
+not apply refuses the whole delta and leaves nothing. Then the swap as for `--apply`, through the updater of
+the new version. A portable copy's browser uses it when the manifest names a delta from its version.
+
+**Deltas of installed copies (P4.1).** Chromium's setup no longer applies patches itself (158 installs only
+from a whole archive), so an installed copy's delta (`*-installed.delta`) is made from and to the files an
+installed copy holds: the browser folder with the release's `setup.exe` at `<version>\Installer\setup.exe`,
+where an installed copy keeps it. `PrepareInstalledDelta` makes the newer files from the `Application`
+folder with it, the newer `setup.exe` included, and gives the command that installs them: that `setup.exe`
+with `--thoron-chrome-bin=<the made folder>` (setup copies it in place of unpacking an archive, its own
+`Installer` folder aside), without starting the browser. The browser does that when the manifest names an
+installed delta from its version; `--apply-installed-delta` does it by hand, keeps the made files in
+`Update` beside `Application` and removes them when the setup has ended. An install for every user is
+never updated from the browser: its installer needs administrator rights, which the updater never asks for.
+`tools/qa/delta_apply_check.py` applies a real delta between two builds and compares every file.
+
+**Rollback (P3.8). `--rollback` says what it would do and does it only with `--confirm`. With
+`--restore-profile <User Data> --snapshots <folder>` it first puts the profile back from the snapshot taken
+before the current version (`before-<version>`, made by `--apply-package --snapshot-profile <User Data>
+--snapshots <folder>`; `snapshot.h`), all or nothing, and then switches the version back.
+`tools/qa/portable_update_check.py` does the round trip: update, a setting changed, rollback; the profile is
+byte for byte the snapshot, the older version's folder byte for byte what it was, and the setting the one
+from before the update.
+
+| Exit code | Meaning |
+|---|---|
+| 0 | Done; with `--check`: up to date |
+| 1 | The command line is not one of the above |
+| 10 | `--status`: no version starts in the folder |
+| 11 | `--apply`: that version is the current one already |
+| 12 | `--apply`: the staged folder is not exactly the files of `files.json`; nothing was switched |
+| 13 | `--apply`: the version is zero, or a path leaves the version's folder |
+| 14 | A rename or a write failed; what was current still is |
+| 15 | `--rollback`: there is no version before the current one |
+| 16 | `--rollback` without `--confirm`: what it would do was said, nothing was done |
+| 17 | `--rollback --restore-profile`: no snapshot from before the current version, or it does not verify; nothing changed |
+| 18 | `--apply-installed-delta`: the newer setup did not install the version (or did not end within 15 minutes) |
+| 20 | `--check`: a newer release for this copy; the downloads follow `full:` and `delta:` |
+| 21 | `--check`: the release cannot be verified (signature, form, channel, replay, dates, downgrade) |
+| 22 | `--check`: a newer release that this updater may not apply; `full:` names the package whose updater applies it, when there is one |
+| 23 | `--check`: a newer release with nothing for this variant and kind |
+
+The modes: `auto` downloads and installs, and the new version starts with the next start; `notify` says a
+newer version is there and installs when asked; `off` looks only when asked. A look the person asks for
+always shows its answer.
+
+## The launcher
+
+`launcher/thoron_launcher_win.cc` becomes `Thoron.exe` of the portable package. It starts the version
+`Application\current` names (or `Application\thoron.exe` of a package made before), with the profile in
+the package's `User Data` folder, which it makes itself: a folder it cannot make would send the browser
+to the computer's own profile folder, so then it starts nothing. It passes its command line on.
+
+It is built without the build's processor flags and links nothing that is built with them (no base, no
+C++ library), so it runs on every x64 processor. On one that cannot run the package's build it says so,
+where the browser itself would stop before any of its code ran. The package names its build in the file
+`variant` (`win-x64-avx2`, `win-x64-sse4`); without it the package is taken for the AVX2 build. Exit
+status: 0 started, 2 no browser in the package, 3 the processor cannot run it, 4 the start failed.
+
+## What it is not yet
+
+- No fetching over the network and no About page: the browser's part (P3.9, P3.20).
+- No place for the public key, the allowed hosts or the stored serial: the caller passes them.
+- One key. Key rotation is P4.7.
+
+## The tests
+
+`manifest_unittest.cc` runs every vector of `manifest_test_vectors.h` and the cases of the stage: a changed
+bit in either file, an older manifest, malformed JSON, an expired manifest. `package_unittest.cc`,
+`swap_unittest.cc` (among them a kill after each step), `snapshot_unittest.cc` and `cli_unittest.cc` test the
+rest. One test takes a snapshot of a copy of a real profile when `THORON_SNAPSHOT_PROFILE_COPY` names one,
+and prints only counts and file names.
+
+    autoninja -C out/<dir> components/thoron_update:thoron_update_all
+    out/<dir>/thoron_update_unittests
+
+The vectors are real. `make_test_vectors.py` makes them with the reference tool and takes its cases from
+the tool's own tests (`tools/update/tests/test_manifest.py`): each manifest, its signature, and what
+`check_update()` makes of it. Most vectors are the example of the spec with one stretch changed, so the
+header holds only the change. To make them again, or to see that the header is what the reference gives:
+
+    py -3 -I make_test_vectors.py --repo <Thoron's repository>
+    py -3 -I make_test_vectors.py --repo <Thoron's repository> --check
+
+Run it again whenever the reference tool, its tests or the spec change.
+
+The key of the vectors is a test key. Its private half is the SHA-256 of a phrase in the script, so it is no
+secret and anyone gets the same vectors. It signs nothing else, and no build of Thoron trusts it. The vector
+of the spec ("A signature to test against") is among them, under its own throwaway key.
